<h1>Orcraft tutorial overview</h1>
<p>This tutorial follows Orcraft's test development experience by hand. It begins by building suites
  with the Test Framework so you can understand Orcraft's principles, structure and approach, then runs
  and inspects those suites from the controller's GUI, drives them from the CLI, and wires them into a
  CI pipeline.</p>

<p>Once those foundations are clear, the same work can be handed to an
  <a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/">AI agent</a>. It writes suites in the same plain-Python
  structure introduced here, runs them on the machines you name and reads the logs, so you can inspect,
  run and extend what it wrote in exactly the same way.</p>

<p>It is based on the Orcraft Tutorial test suite. Before proceeding, complete the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/getting-started/">Getting started</a> guide to install the
  controller and clone the tutorial suite as described there.</p>

<p>All code examples that appear in this tutorial also appear in the tutorial suite so you can
  follow along in the same project, run them locally, and inspect the resulting behavior.</p>

<p>This tutorial covers the framework's most common usages. For complete framework documentation, see
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/">Framework</a>.</p>

<p>To complete the tutorial and see Orcraft's full capabilities, register at least three
  endpoints beforehand - see <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/endpoints/">Adding endpoints</a>.
  Without them, several distributed and multi-endpoint features covered later in the tutorial
  will not be available to try out.</p>
